  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid Sunday with isolated\nafternoon thunderstorms.\n\nHigh pressure to the northeast will move offshore tonight into\nSunday. A stray shower or rumble of thunder could develop over\nparts of the Appalachians late this afternoon into this evening;\notherwise, conditions will be dry. Dry and seasonable \nconditions will become more humid with warmer temperatures on \nSunday. A southerly return flow will bring in this more humid \nairmass; thus, setting the moisture ingredient in the \ndevelopment of a few thunderstorms Sunday afternoon and evening.\nA weak disturbance arriving from the west-northwest on Sunday\ncould be the trigger needed for such convection Sunday afternoon\nand evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Late summer heat, thunderstorm chances return\nnext week.\n\nConfidence remains high that summertime heat returns to the region \nfor most of next week as a strong mid-level ridge builds across the \nsouthern CONUS. Uncertainty remains on exactly where the ridge is \npositioned come the middle of next week, but it certainly looks like \nwe will have highs in the 90s Monday through Thursday and heat \nindices between 95-105. Wednesday looks to be hottest day at this \ntime, with potential for heat advisories across portions of the \nShenandoah Valley and across the DC and Baltimore metro areas into \nSouthern MD. The main caveat for heat impacts is timing of expected \nrounds of convection as MCSs move through/near the region, and any \npotential backdoor fronts that could slide in late in the week.\n\nDisturbances rounding a ridge will steadily move from the Great \nLakes to Mid-Atlantic for most of next week. The timing and \namplitude of these waves have very low predictability beyond a \ncouple days in advance (at best). However, the general theme will be \nenhanced lift and shear clashing with an increasingly hot and \nunstable airmass over or near the Mid-Atlantic. AI/ML guidance has \nbroad elevated probabilities of severe weather much of next week, \nthough focus appears to be shifting to Wednesday and Thursday. \nAgain, timing is going to be a big determining factor in whether any \nsevere weather occurs in the forecast area.\n\nIt should be noted that there is a conditional potential for one or \nmore organized clusters of strong to severe storms at some point \nnext week. The most likely time currently appears to be around mid \nweek, but changes are to be expected at this time range.",
